INT. FENCING HALL - EVENING

A high-ceilinged gymnasium. Strip lights glare on white lamé. Sabres ring against each other on two raised pistes.

SORAYA DELGADO, 20s, hood up, sits alone on a bench taping her blade guard. Across the room, BOHDAN MARCHENKO, 50s, her coach, argues quietly with a judge. HARRIET QUIGLEY, 30s, a rival in a silver jacket, stretches her hamstring on the opposite piste.

Soraya finishes taping. She pulls her mask over her head. She rises. She crosses the floor with an easy stride.

BOHDAN
Soraya. A word.

SORAYA
After the bout.

BOHDAN
Before.

She stops. He crosses to her. He lowers his voice.

BOHDAN (CONT'D)
Harriet is your sister's friend.

SORAYA
I know.

BOHDAN
She has been asking for you.

SORAYA
I know that too.

HARRIET
(calling)
Ready when you are!

Soraya raises a hand to show she's heard. She takes two steps toward the piste and then stops again. She looks at Bohdan.

SORAYA
What is it?

BOHDAN
Your sister called.

She goes still. He looks at his shoes.

SORAYA
Say it.

BOHDAN
She's in hospital. It's not serious. They think.

SORAYA
Why didn't you tell me earlier?

BOHDAN
Because you've waited four years for this bout.

She tears off her mask. She throws it at the bench. It bounces and slides to Harriet's feet. Harriet picks it up, turns it over, and carries it back to her.

HARRIET
Go. I'll tell them you withdrew.

SORAYA
I didn't withdraw.

HARRIET
You did now.

Soraya takes the mask. She holds it a moment, then nods once. She walks out of the hall without looking back.
